  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}\u2061x={sec}^{2}\u2061x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{4}\u2061x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x({sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{2}\u2061x)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot 1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can use $(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x+1} & =\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}\u2061x{sec}^{2}\u2061x-{tan}^{4}\u2061x+1}={cos}^{2}\u2061x.\n$$",
